{"analysis":"This run only listed available gene set libraries. To complete the immune gene panel and downstream analysis, load the Hallmark gene sets using the correct library name—e.g. 

    gs = gp.get_library(name='MSigDB_Hallmark_2020', organism='Human')

—or parse a local Hallmark GMT with `gp.parser.read_gmt()`. Then re-run steps 4–9 to compute immune scores, perform statistical tests, differential expression, and enrichment.","success":false}

{"code":"# Revised Experiment Plan (JSON format)

{
  "hypothesis": "Ulcerated primary melanomas exhibit a suppressed immune gene expression signature compared to non‐ulcerated melanomas.",
  "experiment_plan": {
    "objective": "Test whether ulceration status is associated with downregulation of immune-related genes by (1) reliably loading and verifying immune gene sets, (2) computing immune module scores, (3) performing differential expression and enrichment analyses, and (4) visualizing results.",
    "steps": "1. Data loading and pre-processing:\n   a. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n   b. Subset clinical data to patients with ulceration_indicator in {‘YES’, ‘NO’}, retaining age_at_diagnosis and gender.\n   c. Map expression sample barcodes (first 12 characters) to patient IDs and filter expression matrix.\n2. Immune gene set retrieval and verification:\n   a. Use gseapy.get_library_name(organism='Human') to list available libraries.\n   b. Load the Hallmark library by its exact name (e.g. 'MSigDB_Hallmark_2020').\n   c. Inspect library keys via list(hallmark.keys()) to identify immune sets (those containing 'INFLAMMATORY_RESPONSE', 'INTERFERON', 'TNFA_SIGNALING').\n   d. Fallback: if Hallmark is unavailable, parse a local GMT file (e.g. h.all.v2023.symbols.gmt) by splitting each line on tabs (first field = gene set name, rest = gene symbols).\n3. Immune module score calculation:\n   a. Compile non-redundant genes from selected immune sets; intersect with expression row names.\n   b. Compute per-sample mean expression of immune genes (immune module score).\n4. Statistical comparison of module scores:\n   a. Test normality of immune scores in each group (Shapiro–Wilk).\n   b. Choose two-sample t-test or Wilcoxon rank-sum to compare ulcerated vs. non-ulcerated scores.\n   c. Plot boxplot + points and annotate p-value.\n5. Differential expression of immune genes:\n   a. For each immune gene, fit linear model: expression ~ ulceration_indicator + age_at_diagnosis + gender.\n   b. Extract ulceration coefficient and p-value; adjust p-values (Benjamini–Hochberg).\n   c. Report genes with FDR < 0.05, sorted by log fold change.\n6. Genome-wide differential expression and GSEA:\n   a. Compute t-statistic or log-fold change for all genes between ulcerated and non-ulcerated (optionally adjusting for covariates).\n   b. Create preranked list; run GSEA (gseapy.prerank) using all Hallmark sets or curated immune subset.\n   c. Extract enrichment statistics (ES, NES, p-value, FDR) for immune pathways.\n7. Visualization and reporting:\n   a. Boxplot of immune module scores with p-value.\n   b. Volcano plot for immune genes highlighting significant ones.\n   c. Table of DE immune genes (gene, logFC, p-value, FDR).\n   d. GSEA enrichment table and enrichment plots for top immune pathways.\n8. Sensitivity and robustness checks:\n   a. Recompute scores using median instead of mean.\n   b. Validate with alternative immune signatures (e.g. Reactome 'Immune System' pathway).\n   c. Assess impact of additional covariates (e.g. tumor stage).",
    "deliverables": "1. A validated immune gene panel with documentation of gene-set loading and key name resolution.\n2. Plots: immune module score boxplot (with p-value), volcano plot, GSEA enrichment plots.\n3. Tables: DE immune gene list (logFC, p-value, FDR), GSEA results for immune pathways (ES, NES, p-value, FDR).\n4. A concise report summarizing methods, robustness checks, and interpretation regarding the hypothesis."
  }
}"}
